# HD-186 load harness — configuration template.
# Spec: docs/design/load-capacity-measurement-proposal.md
#
# THE REAL FILE DOES NOT LIVE IN THIS DIRECTORY. Copy it OUT of the repository tree:
#
#   install -m 0600 /dev/null /opt/hamstrack/.loadtest.env
#   $EDITOR /opt/hamstrack/.loadtest.env          # paste the parts this machine needs
#
# The fixture scripts source it themselves ($LOAD_CONFIG, default
# /opt/hamstrack/.loadtest.env); anything already in the environment wins over it. On the
# generator, source it by hand before k6:  set -a; . /opt/hamstrack/.loadtest.env; set +a
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY IT MUST NOT BE `ops/loadtest/config.env`
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `ops/` is a SYNCED path (ops/deploy/synced-paths.txt), and a synced DIRECTORY is replaced
# WHOLESALE by every deploy. A configuration file placed here would therefore:
#
#   * be copied into /opt/hamstrack/.config-backup/<timestamp>/ by apply-config.sh, which
#     keeps the LAST FIVE — so the load password and its hash would sit in five retained
#     copies long after the window;
#   * be reported by ops/drift/hamstrack-config-drift.sh as a file ADDED to a synced
#     directory, raising hamstrack_config_drift{scope="files"} — and "drift reads 0 for
#     every scope" is a HARD PRECONDITION of the window (§5.1 precondition 3). The setup
#     step would trip the gate it is a precondition of, teaching the operator to wave
#     through a failure they caused;
#   * keep ConfigDrift (for: 30m) firing, and that rule is part of §4.3's breach bar via
#     capture/alert-rules.sh — so NO STAGE COULD BE RECORDED AS PASSED. The harness would
#     invalidate its own run.
#
# The same goes for run output: results default to $LOAD_RESULTS_DIR (/var/tmp/hd186), not
# to ops/loadtest/results/.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AND DO NOT NAME IT `.env` OR `.env.anything`
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ops/deploy/apply-config.sh refuses, as a hard error, any manifest entry that would place
# a file whose basename is `.env` or starts with `.env.`:
#
#     .env|.env.*|Caddyfile)  die "... are NEVER synced ..."
#
# That refusal is per FILE PLACED, not per manifest line, precisely so a `.env` committed
# INSIDE a synced directory cannot slip through. It exists to stop a deploy overwriting the
# box's own `.env` (its secrets, APP_IMAGE_TAG, APP_MEMORY_LIMIT, SITE_ADDRESS — the single
# rollback story depends on that file surviving every deploy).
#
# `config.env.example` and `.loadtest.env` both sit outside that pattern. `.env.example`
# would match it, and it would not break this harness — it would break EVERY DEPLOY OF THE
# PRODUCT, at the apply step, for a filename choice in a load-testing directory.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SPLIT IT BY MACHINE. NEITHER MACHINE NEEDS THE WHOLE FILE.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   [BOX]        the fixture and the capture. Needs the bcrypt HASH, the database
#                connection and the container names. It does NOT need the plaintext
#                password: the one script here that logs in (verify-api.sh) PROMPTS for it,
#                for one command.
#
#                BOTH HALVES OF THE BOX ACTUALLY READ THIS FILE NOW. For a long time only
#                fixture/lib.sh sourced it, so APP_CONTAINER / PG_CONTAINER / PROM_CONTAINER
#                were read by nothing that had loaded the file that sets them — an operator
#                who followed this template exactly got "FATAL no container named
#                'hamstrack-app-1'" while looking at the correct name in their own
#                configuration. capture/lib-config.sh is the capture side's half.
#   [GENERATOR]  k6. Needs the PLAINTEXT password and the base URL. It does NOT need the
#                hash, the DSN, the container names or the confirmation.
#   [BOTH]       the run id, which is part of the fixture's slugs.
#
# One file carrying both halves to both machines doubles the number of places each secret
# exists for no benefit to either.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CREATE THE GENERATOR'S COPY THE SAME WAY, AND DO NOT COPY THE WHOLE FILE TO IT
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The instructions at the top create the BOX's copy. The generator needs its own, and it was
# never given a creation step — which in practice means somebody scp's the box's file,
# putting the bcrypt hash and the database connection on a second machine that has no use
# for either.
#
#   install -m 0600 /dev/null /opt/hamstrack/.loadtest.env      # on the GENERATOR
#   $EDITOR /opt/hamstrack/.loadtest.env                        # [GENERATOR] + [BOTH] only
#   set -a; . /opt/hamstrack/.loadtest.env; set +a              # before running k6
#
# On the generator that is: BASE_URL, HOST_HEADER, LOAD_PASSWORD, LOAD_RUN_ID,
# LOAD_RESULTS_DIR, TOKENS_FILE, LADDER/RAMP/HOLD and the entitlements. Nothing else.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHERE  [GENERATOR], and [BOX] for verify-api.sh
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The ORIGIN, not the edge. The production site is proxied by Cloudflare; a sustained
# synthetic burst through the edge measures Cloudflare's caching, connection reuse and bot
# handling, and risks being challenged by a system whose configuration is not ours. Point
# this at the origin and set HOST_HEADER to the public hostname, so the request path is
# generator -> Caddy -> app: the same path a real request takes, minus the edge.
#
# RECORD THAT THE EDGE IS EXCLUDED. The published capacity is ORIGIN capacity; Cloudflare
# only ever adds latency and removes load, so an origin number is the conservative one.
#
# Every HTTP client in the harness reads BASE_URL and sends HOST_HEADER — k6 and
# verify-api.sh alike. If you find one that does not, that is a defect, not a convention.
BASE_URL=http://10.0.0.5:8080
HOST_HEADER=

# Local development / rehearsal:
# BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
# HOST_HEADER=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATABASE  [BOX]   (the fixture generator only — k6 never touches the database)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# dsn    — psql on this machine connects to LOAD_DB_DSN
# docker — psql runs inside LOAD_PG_CONTAINER (production: 5432 is not published, and
#          publishing it for the run would be a configuration change to the box under
#          measurement)
#
# NEITHER OF THESE ANSWERS "WHICH DATABASE" — see LOAD_TARGET below, which does.
LOAD_PSQL_MODE=docker
LOAD_PG_CONTAINER=hamstrack-postgres-1
LOAD_DB_USER=hamstrack
LOAD_DB_NAME=hamstrack

# Used only when LOAD_PSQL_MODE=dsn, and by capture.sh in LOAD_CAPTURE_MODE=host.
# NOTE for local runs on this project's dev box: port 15432, not 5432 (CLAUDE.md — 5432 is
# taken by a native PostgreSQL install).
#
# NO PASSWORD IN THE DSN. capture.sh in host mode runs `psql "$LOAD_DB_DSN"` once every
# INTERVAL seconds for the whole window, so an inline password is published through
# /proc/<pid>/cmdline to every local account, several hundred times — the same finding that
# was fixed in verify-api.sh, arriving by a different door. capture/lib-config.sh REFUSES a
# DSN matching `://user:password@`; put the secret in LOAD_DB_PASSWORD and it writes a
# mode-0600 PGPASSFILE for psql to read, or export PGPASSFILE yourself.
# LOAD_DB_DSN=postgresql://hamstrack@localhost:15432/hamstrack
# LOAD_DB_PASSWORD=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE LOAD PASSWORD   hash: [BOX] · plaintext: [GENERATOR]
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# EVERY load account shares one password. It is NOT in this repository, and that is a
# decision rather than an omission: a bcrypt hash committed here would be a credential in a
# SOURCE-AVAILABLE repository with a publicly known plaintext, and every account reachable
# with it would exist on production during a window in which the instance is deliberately
# being pushed to its limits.
#
# GENERATE IT. DO NOT CHOOSE IT. The residual risk is not bounded by the window: an aborted
# run leaves the accounts in place until fixture/revoke.sh is run, so the entropy here is
# protecting production accounts for as long as it takes somebody to notice.
#
#   PW=$(openssl rand -base64 24)                            # 32 characters; floor is 24
#   htpasswd -bnBC 12 "" "$PW" | tr -d ':\n' | sed 's/^\$2y/\$2a/'
#
# SecurityConfig uses BCryptPasswordEncoder(12), so the hash must be at strength 12. The
# 24-character floor is enforced by mint-tokens.js and verify-api.sh, which are where each
# machine first sees the plaintext.
#
# LOAD_PASSWORD_HASH goes in the BOX's file. LOAD_PASSWORD goes in the GENERATOR's.
#
# QUOTE THE HASH WITH SINGLE QUOTES, as below. This file is SOURCED by every script here,
# under `set -u`, and a bcrypt hash ALWAYS contains `$`. Unquoted, `$2a$12$…` is read as
# positional parameters and the run dies with `line 5: $2: unbound variable` — a message
# that names neither this file nor quoting, on a value that looks correct in `cat`. The
# same applies to any value added later that can contain `$`; the plaintext password is
# generated from base64, whose alphabet cannot, so it needs no quotes.
LOAD_PASSWORD=
LOAD_PASSWORD_HASH=''

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE RUN HANDLE  [BOTH]
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A random suffix that becomes part of the fixture's workspace slugs
# (hd186-load-<run-id>-a). Without it the handle is `hd186-load-`, which is a string a USER
# can produce by naming a workspace — and a collision means either a teardown that refuses
# and strands 750 000 rows mid-window, or an issue_seq resync writing a real tenant's
# counter. A four-byte run id cannot be arrived at by naming anything.
#
#   LOAD_RUN_ID=$(openssl rand -hex 4)
#
# RECORD IT IN THE RUN RECORD. generate.sh requires it; teardown falls back to the base
# prefix without it (losing the ability to clean up is the worse failure) and excludes any
# matched workspace holding a real member.
LOAD_RUN_ID=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE TWO CONFIRMATIONS  [BOX]
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ops/ is a SYNCED path, so this harness arrives on production with every deploy (§11 Q7).
# Being PRESENT on the box is not the same as being PERMITTED to run there, and these are
# what say so. Set BOTH on the command line, in the window — not here:
#
#   LOAD_CONFIRM=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d) LOAD_TARGET=hamstrack bash fixture/generate.sh
#
# LOAD_CONFIRM  must equal TODAY'S date in UTC. A confirmation that outlives its window is
#               not a confirmation.
# LOAD_TARGET   must equal what the SERVER says its database is called (current_database()),
#               read back over the same connection that is about to be written to. Every
#               other variable here CHOOSES the target and therefore cannot check it — and
#               that gap is what once let a "rehearsal" run against production.
LOAD_CONFIRM=
LOAD_TARGET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE FIXTURE  [BOX]
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Deterministic: two runs with the same seed produce identical row counts and identical id
# ordering. RECORD IT IN THE RUN RECORD (§4.9) — a re-run at a different seed is a new
# experiment, not a re-measurement.
LOAD_SEED=0.4242

# 1.0 = the §4.2 shape (~40 000 issues in workspace A, ~750 000 rows, an estimated
# 250-350 MB with indexes — the generator PRINTS the measured size, and where the estimate
# and the measurement disagree the measurement wins).
#
# THE REHEARSAL IGNORES THIS. rehearse.sh forces its own small scale (LOAD_REHEARSAL_SCALE,
# default 0.01) rather than defaulting it, precisely because this line exists and a
# rehearsal that inherited 1.0 would generate a full fixture wherever it was pointed.
LOAD_SCALE=1.0

# §5.1 precondition 5: free disk >= 5 GB and >= 3x the projected fixture. The volume was
# grown 8 -> 20 GiB before this ticket, so this should pass comfortably; it is checked
# anyway, because a full disk is the ONLY failure in this plan that can damage real data.
#
# THIS IS THE BOX'S DISK. The generator checks its own, separately, in run-ladder.sh — for
# its captures, not for the database. The two are different machines and are not redundant.
LOAD_MIN_FREE_MB=5120
LOAD_DISK_PATH=/

# Where output goes, on BOTH machines. Outside the synced tree, for the reason at the top
# of this file.
LOAD_RESULTS_DIR=/var/tmp/hd186

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE RUN  [GENERATOR]
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Workspace B's id. NO LONGER AN INPUT: it is resolved in setup() from a workspace-B
# principal in tokens.json, asserted to be foreign to the A principal and proved to exist
# by that B principal (k6/lib/fixture.js). Set it only if you want the CROSS-CHECK — a
# mismatch is then fatal, which is how a stale id from a previous fixture is caught instead
# of quietly making every canary request 404 for the boring reason.
#
# A STALE VALUE HERE IS EXIT 1, NOT EXIT 7. setup() refuses before any measured request, so
# the run is a HARNESS refusal — it says nothing about tenancy and must not be written up as
# a failed canary. Pointing this at a workspace you ARE a member of therefore tests nothing.
# The sabotage that does exercise the canary is `canaryWsId: wsB.id` -> `wsA.id` in
# k6/lib/fixture.js (README §3), which exits 7.
CANARY_WORKSPACE_ID=

# ABSOLUTE, AND OUTSIDE THE REPOSITORY. Two separate reasons, both load-bearing:
#
#   * k6's open() resolves a relative path against the SCRIPT that calls it (k6/lib/auth.js)
#     while handleSummary's write resolves against the WORKING DIRECTORY, so the same
#     relative name means two different files at the two ends. mint-tokens.js now REFUSES a
#     relative TOKENS_FILE rather than defaulting to one that is right from one directory.
#   * it is a CREDENTIAL FILE for the whole load pool — access tokens accepted on a
#     signature check, and refresh tokens good for thirty days. config.env and results/ were
#     both moved out of the synced ops/ tree for weaker reasons than that, and this one file
#     was left inside it.
#
# It defaults to $LOAD_RESULTS_DIR/tokens.json.
TOKENS_FILE=/var/tmp/hd186/tokens.json

# The ladder. Each stage is its own k6 run, with its own thresholds and its own exit code.
# QUOTED. This file is read with `set -a; . file`, so an unquoted list makes the shell try
# to run `2` as a command ("2: command not found") and leaves LADDER empty — a whole
# measurement reduced to run-ladder.sh's built-in default by a missing pair of quotes, with
# one line of noise as the only warning.
LADDER="1 2 5 10 15 20 30 45 60 80 100"
RAMP=1m
HOLD=4m

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE ACCOUNT POOL  [GENERATOR, and the fixture's own numbers must match]
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Three address families, minted separately (k6/mint-tokens.js) and generated separately
# (fixture/10-generate.sql section 1). k6 maps a VU to a principal INJECTIVELY —
# accountsA[__VU - 1], no modulo — so ACCOUNTS must cover every VU k6 ALLOCATES, which is
# the sum over the mix's scenarios and NOT the ladder's top VU count. The browsing mix
# declares three scenarios and instantiates 2xVUS+1, so a 100-VU top stage needs 201.
#
# Both run-ladder.sh (asking k6 inspect --execution-requirements) and each mix's setup()
# REFUSE rather than warn when the pool is short. Changing these means REGENERATING THE
# FIXTURE — the accounts have to exist before they can be minted.
ACCOUNTS=210
ACCOUNTS_CANARY=2
ACCOUNTS_B=15

# Between stages, run k6/refresh-tokens.js when the access tokens would otherwise expire
# mid-stage. Refresh tokens are SINGLE-USE (/api/auth/refresh rotates), so a stage that runs
# past the expiry spends the whole file's chain and leaves every LATER stage unable to
# authenticate — which is the upper half of the ladder, where the capacity number comes
# from. 0 makes run-ladder.sh refuse instead of advancing the chain for you.
LOAD_AUTO_REFRESH=1

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE ENTITLEMENTS  [GENERATOR] — AND THEY MUST EQUAL WHAT THE BOX IS RUNNING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Probe P1 asks whether the instance absorbs ONE fully-entitled principal without degrading
# everybody else. "Fully entitled" is a RATE, and the rate is the per-principal budget the
# APPLICATION is configured with: SEARCH_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE and REPORTS_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE
# in the app container's environment. capture/fingerprint.sh prints both — READ THEM AND SET
# THESE TO MATCH.
#
# If they disagree, the probe still runs and still passes or fails; it just measures a
# different entitlement than the one the box grants, and nothing in the output says so.
# These are the defaults the product ships.
SEARCH_ENTITLEMENT=120
REPORT_ENTITLEMENT=60

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CONTAINER NAMES, CAPTURE MODE AND THE ABORT THRESHOLDS  [BOX]
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# READ THE NAMES OFF `docker ps` — compose's project-name prefix differs between a checkout
# directory and /opt/hamstrack. capture.sh refuses to start on a name that does not exist,
# because the samplers swallow per-sample failures and a typo otherwise produces a whole
# window of HD186_SCRAPE_FAILED that is discovered while reading the results.

# docker (compose deployment) or host (the JAR under systemd, no containers). Not detected —
# a mode guessed from "is Docker installed" produces a different capture on a machine that
# has Docker for something else.
#
# PIN THIS TO `docker` FOR THE MEASUREMENT WINDOW. `host` is a real code path and it is
# UNEXERCISED: nothing in this window needs it, it is the only path that runs a local psql
# (so the only one where a database credential can reach a command line at all), and its
# fail-fast was broken until 2026-08-29 in a way that fired precisely when the actuator was
# working. Use it to size a self-hosted box outside a window, and record that it was
# unexercised when you do.
LOAD_CAPTURE_MODE=docker

# ONE NAME PER THING, AND THE LOAD_-PREFIXED ONE IS IT.
#
# capture/lib-config.sh resolves `PG_CONTAINER := ${PG_CONTAINER:-${LOAD_PG_CONTAINER:-…}}`,
# so a bare PG_CONTAINER set here WINS over LOAD_PG_CONTAINER — which is the opposite of what
# the fixture side documents. This template used to ship both, so editing the line the
# comment calls "the source" changed the fixture and not the capture, silently, on the one
# machine where they must agree. Only the LOAD_-prefixed names are set now; the bare ones
# remain as a fallback in code for an operator who exports them deliberately.
LOAD_APP_CONTAINER=hamstrack-app-1
LOAD_PROM_CONTAINER=hamstrack-prometheus-1
# LOAD_PG_CONTAINER is set in the DATABASE section above — one name, one place.

# The actuator, scraped every INTERVAL seconds. In `docker` mode this URL is resolved INSIDE
# the app container, so localhost is the app itself; in `host` mode it is fetched from the
# box, where the management port must be reachable. Host mode REQUIRES it and this file used
# to describe it in prose without ever assigning it.
ACTUATOR_URL=http://localhost:8080/actuator/prometheus
# PROM_URL is only used by capture/export-prometheus.sh.
# PROM_URL=http://localhost:9090

# Sampling period for capture.sh and watchdog.sh, in seconds.
INTERVAL=5

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE ABORT THRESHOLDS — THE NUMBERS THAT DECIDE WHEN THE RUN IS STOPPED
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# watchdog.sh writes a STOP FILE when one of these is crossed, and run-ladder.sh honours it
# between stages (LOAD_ABORT_FILE). They are §5.3's abort conditions expressed as numbers,
# and they were in NEITHER this template nor the README — so the three values that decide
# whether a measurement window ends early were reachable only by reading watchdog.sh.
# The values below are its defaults; raising one is a decision to record in the run record.
# condition 1: free disk on the box. The only one of the three that can damage real data.
DISK_FLOOR_MB=500
# condition 4: host available memory.
MEM_FLOOR_MB=150
# condition 4: swap in use. With swap configured, memory exhaustion presents as LATENCY
# rather than as a dead container, which is why it has a number of its own.
SWAP_CEILING_MB=512

# The GENERATOR's own free-disk floor, checked by run-ladder.sh before each stage. It is NOT
# a second opinion on the box's disk: it is about losing the evidence for the stage being
# run, not about losing data.
# GEN_MIN_FREE_MB=500

# If the box-side stop file is reachable from the generator (shared mount, rsync, SSM copy),
# name it here and run-ladder.sh will stop between stages on it. Unset, it is not assumed.
# LOAD_ABORT_FILE=/var/tmp/hd186/window/ABORT
